{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He inherited his title from his father Raymond II after an assassination attempt by the Hashashin group left his father dead. The young count faced immediate challenges because the kingdom of Jerusalem did not trust him fully due to his family history. He spent many years as a prisoner in Damascus after being captured by Nur ad-Din. This long captivity shaped his political views and made him cautious in his dealings with the Muslim powers surrounding his lands.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond was born in the early 1140s and became the ruler of Tripoli at a very young age. His father died in 1152 during a failed ambush by the Ismaili sect known as the Assassins. The local nobility supported Raymond despite his youth because he was the legitimate heir. The situation remained tense until he reached adulthood and could govern effectively. He managed to secure his position by negotiating with powerful neighbors while maintaining the independence of his county. His rule brought a period of stability to a region that had suffered from constant warfare and internal strife.\n\n*Conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nTension existed between Raymond and the kings of Jerusalem throughout his reign. King Baldwin III viewed him with suspicion because of his father's death and his time as a prisoner. Raymond often acted independently and sometimes refused to join royal armies in battle. This behavior caused friction but also allowed him to preserve his own resources. He eventually became regent for the young King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. This role placed him at the center of political power within the Crusader states. He tried to balance the interests of various factions while protecting his own territory from external threats.\n\n*Role in the Fall of 